    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.1038/s41597-026-06918-9",
    "abstract": "Permafrost, which underlies roughly one-fifth of global land area, is highly vulnerable to ongoing climate warming and its degradation has important implications for hydrological processes, carbon cycling, ecosystems, and infrastructure stability. It remains challenging to accurately project its future distribution due to various factors. The Surface Frost Index (SFI) threshold is a critical parameter that captures first-order thermal forcing for projecting future permafrost distribution. Here, the future global annual frozen ground distribution datasets are produced using an optimized frost number threshold (F<sub>at</sub>) calibrated via kappa coefficient-based accuracy assessment. By comparing simulated outputs with benchmark maps, we identify the optimal F<sub>at</sub>. Applying this threshold to downscaled CMIP6 data (0.25\u00b0 resolution), we generate annual frozen ground distributions (2020-2099) under four SSP scenarios (SSP126, SSP245, SSP370, SSP585). The datasets reveal accelerated permafrost degradation, with mid-century (2040-2060) losses of 19 \u00b1 3% to 28 \u00b1 3% and late-century (2080-2099) losses escalating to 21 \u00b1 3% to 61 \u00b1 5%, peaking under SSP585. These high-resolution projections provide critical insights for assessing climate impacts and guiding cryospheric adaptation strategies.",
    "field": "Frozen ground modeling",
    "problem": "Accurately projecting future permafrost distribution is challenging because the critical diagnostic threshold in the Frost Number Model is uncertain and varies across studies, and a clear method for determining this threshold accurately remains lacking.",
    "method": "This paper presents a novel method to project frozen ground distributions by optimizing the Frost Number Model's threshold, which is calibrated by comparing simulated outputs with benchmark maps using a Kappa coefficient-based accuracy assessment to determine the optimal value.",
    "task": "The task is to generate future global annual datasets of frozen ground distribution (2020-2099) using a Frost Number Model, where a key threshold is optimized with a Kappa coefficient and applied to downscaled CMIP6 climate projections under multiple scenarios.",
